15. What is a transaction?
    A transaction is a unit of work consisting of one or more database operations that are treated as a single logical unit. Either all operations are successfully completed, or none of them are applied.

16. ACID
    A — Atomicity
    A transaction is all or nothing. Either all operations are committed, or all are rolled back.

    C — Consistency
    A transaction takes the database from one valid state to another valid state while maintaining database rules and constraints.
    Your “not in between” idea is useful for remembering it, but valid state → valid state is the better interview wording.
    I — Isolation
    One transaction should not interfere with another concurrent transaction.
    D — Durability
    Once a transaction is committed, the data remains saved even if there is a database/server crash or restart.

17. commit :-Commit means permanently save all the changes made by a transaction to the database.

18. Rollback :- ROLLBACK is used to undo the changes made by a transaction that have not been committed.

19. Save point :- Savepoint is used to create a checkpoint inside a transaction, so you can rollback to that point without rolling back the entire transaction.

20. Isolation levels:-
    | Isolation Level      | Easy Memory                      |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| **Read Uncommitted** | Can read uncommitted data        |
| **Read Committed**   | Only committed data              |
| **Repeatable Read**  | Same row → same value            |
| **Serializable**     | Transactions behave sequentially |


21. Dirty Read :- A dirty read occurs when one transaction reads data that has been modified by another transaction but has not yet been committed.

22. Non-repeatable read:- Within the same transaction, you read the same row twice and get different values because another transaction modified and committed it in between.

23. Phantom read:- Phantom Read is when a transaction executes the same query twice, but the second execution returns new or fewer rows because another transaction inserted, deleted, or changed rows that match the condition.

24. Optimistic locking
    Optimistic locking is a concurrency-control mechanism where we assume conflicts are rare. The application maintains a version number for the record. When a transaction updates the record, it checks whether the version is still the same. If another transaction has already updated it, the version changes and the update fails.

	the second transaction doesn't necessarily "see" the new version. It attempts an update using its old version, and the database/JPA detects that the version no longer matches. In JPA, this commonly results in an OptimisticLockException.

	“If an OptimisticLockException occurs, I can retry the operation using the latest version, or return a conflict to the client and ask them to refresh. The choice depends on whether the operation can be safely retried.”

25. Pessimistic locking
    Pessimistic locking assumes that conflicts are likely to happen. So, when one transaction accesses a row for an update, it locks that row. Other transactions trying to modify the same row have to wait until the lock is released.

26. Why don't we always use SERIALIZABLE?
    We don't always use SERIALIZABLE because although it provides the strongest isolation and prevents concurrency anomalies, it can significantly reduce concurrency by causing more blocking and waiting. Therefore, we choose the lowest isolation level that provides the consistency required by the business.

Note:- More isolation → More blocking → Less performance/concurrency.

