33. partitionining
    Partitioning is a database technique where a large table is divided into smaller, manageable pieces called partitions, while logically it still remains one table.
	The main purpose is to improve query performance and manage large amounts of data.

	Range Partitioning → based on a range of values
    Example: dates/year
    List Partitioning → based on specific values
    Example: country = India, USA
    Hash Partitioning → database uses a hash function to distribute rows
    Example: distributing customers across partitions.


34. sharding
    Sharding is a database scaling technique where data is horizontally distributed across multiple independent database servers, called shards.
	Unlike partitioning, the data is not just divided within one database server — it is distributed across multiple database instances/servers.

	Range-based Sharding      → Range of values (Data is divided based on a range of the shard key)
    Hash-based Sharding       → Hash function (A hash function is applied to the shard key to determine the shard.)
    Directory-based Sharding  → Mapping tells you the shard (A lookup/directory service maintains the mapping between the shard key and the shard.)


35. partitioning vs sharding
    | Partitioning                           | Sharding                                   |
| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Divides data into partitions           | Distributes data across servers            |
| Usually within a database system       | Across multiple database instances/servers |
| Mainly data organization/performance   | Mainly horizontal scaling                  |
| One database system manages partitions | Multiple database servers                  |

Partitioning → Big table → smaller pieces
Sharding → Big database → multiple database servers

36. Why would you choose sharding over partitioning?
    I would choose sharding when a single database server has reached its scalability limits. Partitioning divides data within a database system, whereas sharding distributes data across multiple database servers, allowing horizontal scaling of storage and workload."

